## **7. INFORMATION-GEOMETRIC PERSPECTIVE**

### **7.1 Fisher Information and Metric Compatibility**

**Definition 7.1 (Fisher Information Metric).**

On the probability simplex $\Delta^{k-1} = \{\mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}^k : p_i \geq 0, \sum p_i = 1\}$, the Fisher Information metric is:

$$g_{ij}^{\text{Fisher}} = \frac{\partial^2}{\partial p_i \partial p_j} D_{\text{KL}}(\mathbf{p} \| \mathbf{q}) = \begin{cases}
\frac{1}{p_i} & \text{if } i = j \\
0 & \text{if } i \neq j
\end{cases}$$

so $g^{\text{Fisher}} = \text{diag}(1/p_1, \ldots, 1/p_k)$.

**Theorem 7.1 (Metric Compatibility with KL Divergence).**

The product metric on $\mathcal{M}_{\text{safe}}$:

$$g = g_{\text{sem}} \oplus g_{\text{log}} \oplus g_{\text{Euclidean}}$$

is compatible with the KL divergence in the sense that:

$$\langle \nabla_g D_{\text{KL}}(\mathbf{p} \| \mathbf{q}), v \rangle_g = dD_{\text{KL}}(\mathbf{p})[v]$$

for all tangent vectors $v \in T_\mathbf{p} \Delta_\epsilon$.

**Proof.**

The directional derivative of KL divergence is:

$$d D_{\text{KL}}(\mathbf{p})[v] = \sum_i v_i (\log p_i - \log q_i)$$

The Riemannian gradient with respect to the Euclidean metric on $\Delta_\epsilon$ is:

$$\nabla_g D_{\text{KL}} = \text{grad}_{\text{Euclidean}}(D_{\text{KL}}) = \begin{pmatrix} \log p_1 - \log q_1 \\ \vdots \\ \log p_k - \log q_k \end{pmatrix}$$

Thus:

$$\langle \nabla_g D_{\text{KL}}, v \rangle_g = \sum_i v_i (\log p_i - \log q_i) = dD_{\text{KL}}[v]$$

so the metric is compatible. □

**Information-Geometric Interpretation:**

The KL divergence can be viewed as the "divergence" between two probability distributions measured in the Fisher-Rao metric (the canonical metric on the space of probability distributions). By using the Euclidean metric on the restricted simplex with $p_i \geq \epsilon$, we get a Lipschitz approximation to the Fisher-Rao metric, preserving the information-geometric structure while avoiding singularities.

