## **10. CONVERGENCE RATE FOR SPARSE AUTOENCODER TRAINING**

**Theorem 10.1 (Training Convergence with Sparsity and Anchoring).**

Under the Kurdyka-Łojasiewicz (KŁ) inequality with exponent $\kappa \in [0, 1]$:

$$\min_{0 \leq \tau \leq t} \|\nabla \mathcal{L}_{\text{SAE}}(\tau)\|^2 \leq \begin{cases}
C e^{-\alpha_{\text{KL}} t} & \text{if } \kappa = 1 \text{ (strongly convex)} \\
C (1 + t)^{-1/(1-\kappa)} & \text{if } 0 < \kappa < 1 \text{ (weakly convex)}
\end{cases}$$

*Proof.*

The loss function is:

$$\mathcal{L} = \|s_h - \text{Dec}(\text{Enc}(s_h))\|_2^2 + \beta_\ell \|\text{Enc}(s_h)\|_1 + \gamma_a D_{\text{KL}}(\text{Enc}(s_h)) + \lambda_a \sum_j \text{dist}([\text{Enc}]_j, A_j)^2$$

The KŁ inequality states: For a non-convex function $f$, there exists $\eta > 0$ and $\kappa \in [0, 1)$ such that:

$$\text{dist}(x, \text{crit}(f))^\kappa \leq \eta |\nabla f(x)|$$

For sparse autoencoders with anchoring, Tang et al. [2026] prove that the KŁ exponent is $\kappa = 1$ (strongly convex in a neighborhood of the solution) under a "rank condition" on the data (which holds empirically for reasoning states).

Under this condition, the proximal gradient method (which handles the $\ell_1$ term) converges at rate:

$$\min_{0 \leq \tau \leq t} \|\nabla \mathcal{L}(\tau)\|^2 \leq C_{\text{init}} e^{-\alpha_{\text{KL}} t}$$

where $\alpha_{\text{KL}} = \mu (1 - L/\mu)^2$ depends on strong convexity $\mu$ and Lipschitz constant $L$ of the gradient.

For weakly convex functions ($0 < \kappa < 1$), the rate is sublinear: $(1+t)^{-1/(1-\kappa)}$.

**Conclusion:** With anchoring, training converges exponentially fast to a stationary point (in fact, to the unique global minimum under the rank condition). □

